<a href="#"><img alt="P1" src="https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p1.svg?v=9" align="top"></a> **Partial listing cached on child-resolve failure; `forceResolve` cannot recover it**

`resolveListingChildren` stores the result of `resolveChildren` (even a partial one) at `{ ipnsName, sequenceNumber }`. If any child's resolve throws (transient network error, ROT-07 rejection, bad AEAD), that child is silently dropped and the incomplete `ResolvedChild[]` is cached. Every subsequent call to `listFolder` or `ensureFolderLoaded` — including the `{ forceResolve: true }` path used by the 30 s poll (`invalidateOpenFolder`) and nav re-resolve (`refreshFolderListing`) — checks `cached.sequenceNumber === sequenceNumber`. Unless a remote write advances the folder's IPNS sequence, the same partial listing is returned on every poll/nav cycle: the missing file stays invisible for the entire session.

The only recovery paths today are (a) making a write to the folder (advancing its sequenceNumber, which invalidates the cache entry) or (b) a page reload (destroying the in-memory `listingCache`). Neither happens automatically on network recovery.

A targeted fix: skip the `listingCache.set` call whenever `resolveChildren` dropped at least one child (compare `resolved.length < children.length`). That way a later call with a recovered network — even at the same sequenceNumber — will reattempt the failed children rather than reusing the incomplete entry.

